

FIRST Check 

git config user.name && git config user.email

git config user.name "Aarti Kanojia"
git config user.email "girlsmilewith.kanojia@gmail.com"


STEP 1 — Generate SSH key for girlsmilewith (if not already)

If you already have one, skip this.

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "girlsmilewith.kanojia@gmail.com"

When asked for file name, enter:
~/.ssh/id_ed25519_girlsmilewith

 STEP 2 — Add Key to SSH Agent

 eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_girlsmilewith

 STEP 3 — Add Public Key to GitHub (girlsmilewith account)

Copy the key:
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_girlsmilewith.pub

Then:

GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG Keys → New SSH Key → Paste → Save

STEP 4 — Create SSH Config (IMPORTANT)

Open:
nano ~/.ssh/config

Add this block:
# Mohit account (default)
Host github-mohit
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

# Girlsmilewith account
Host github-girlsmilewith
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_girlsmilewith

    Save (Ctrl+X → Y → Enter)

     STEP 5 — Update creatorpilot Remote

Go inside creatorpilot repo:
cd /path/to/creatorpilot
Check current remote:
git remote -v

Now change it to use girlsmilewith SSH alias:
git remote set-url origin git@github-girlsmilewith:aartikanojia/creatorpilot.git

(Replace repo path if needed)

 STEP 6 — Test It

 ssh -T git@github-girlsmilewith
 You should see:
 Hi girlsmilewith! You've successfully authenticated.

